WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:05.430 align:start position:0%
 
[Music]  hey everybody time once again for my  tomes of terror book review series  so  several weeks back maybe it was a month  i can't really remember exactly  i was contacted through email by an  independent author named christina  mirzoy i hope i'm pronouncing that right  if i'm not i apologize  uh but she contacted me and asked if i  would review her book the headsman  which actually came out  it was published in january of 2022 i  think and it was also available to read  for free on kindle unlimited which is  really good service and i use it a lot  for these book reviews  so i started doing a little bit of  background research into christina  mirzoy  and that led me to discover some kind of  like interesting things about her she's  actually a very new emerging writer  she's a young romanian woman  who actually speaks four languages but  mainly at least so far reads and writes  in english not only because i was  reading an interview with her and she  said that she finds it's what she calls  english's rich vocabulary um more  suitable to expressing herself like what  you know the stories she wants to write  but also she said because a lot of her  favorite authors such as ray bradbury um  are american or british so she read a  lot of that american or british like  fantasy like growing up  so she's very very influenced by dark  fantasy greek mythology  gothic literature  weird fiction  and fairy tales probably most  prominently all of those elements are  very very readily apparent in her book  the headsman  now the book only clocks in at a little  over 40 pages 41 pages 42 pages  somewhere around there but i have to say  that it actually really does pack a lot  of  this just really creepy like uncanny  kind of atmosphere and a lot of real  chilling like intrigue into a very small  space  and i will also say that this is  definitely a story you'll want to read  through a few times um to kind of catch  all the intricate details that are  interwoven in there  in form it's not really a novel  necessarily nor is it a short story it  reads something like a collection of  short stories but they're all  interrelated  and each story or chapter i guess if you  want to call it that is told from the  perspective of a different character so  the action takes place in true grimm's  fairy tale fashion  in some sort of quasi-mystical perhaps  medieval village  in no concrete time or place  the first story which is told from the  point of view of the headsman in other  words the village's executioner  is called the witch  and it describes sort of like the  headsman's infatuation with the  beautiful wife of the duke uh you know  of the village  and this headsman is eventually called  on to execute her after she is accused  of witchcraft  so from here on out each story kind of  fills in  more and more particulars about this  village and its people  and with each kind of like shift in  viewpoint and sometimes in time frame  because i think it does kind of like go  back in time sometimes as one of the  characters is like talking about  something that happened in their past  um you get kind of like further and  further drawn into this kind of  grim web of deceit and betrayal and kind  of like all these complicated things  going on like among all of the townsfolk  now none of the characters are ever  named they're only ever referred to by  their i guess their station in life  for example the flower girl  the young fool the merchant the big man  the duke the mother the maid  the servant boy and the martyr  there's also as far as i can recall no  dialogue uh everything is laid out in a  very matter-of-fact very elegant prose  much like as i mentioned a fairy tale or  like kind of sort of like an unsettling  legend or series of legends kind of told  around a campfire type of tone to it now  the pretty genius thing about the way  the book is presented  is that while each story or story  fragment whatever you want to call it is  quite beautiful and really evocative and  very magically spooky like all on its  own  each successive story actually builds on  the last one  so what you learned about  you know for example what you learned  about the woman selling flowers in the  first tale which was told from the  headsman's perspective the next one is  actually told from her perspective and  she goes back in time and tells a little  bit about that so everything you thought  you knew about her from the first story  that was from the husband's perspective  is sort of like upended and  re-contextualized  by the next story so it kind of goes on  like that where it's like things are set  up like one person's perspective is said  and then you kind of get come at it from  a different angle and like see it from  you know maybe the person who was the  victim in this or maybe like another  person that was involved that knows some  information that this person didn't know  and stuff like that  so you also learn for example again how  the duke's wife the supposed witch ended  up in her predicament like how she ended  up getting executed and all the other  kind of  subterfuge and knavery and all this kind  of stuff that's going on behind the  scenes of this  kind of i'm not gonna lie like this very  mean and like vindictive little village  and i will say too that despite the  book's brief run time uh i know that's  usually just made for movies but it  applies here as well the language that  she uses is so  concise and like so descriptive  that it kind of feels as though there's  just whole  oceans of implication and emotion and  stuff like going on behind the pages you  know what i mean it's it really is  evocative of a much larger world even  though it's like very very short um  almost kind of like a long form prose  poem i think like the the prose is very  straightforward it's not but it is very  like i said she's she's just very good  with language and of kind of implying  like a much larger world beyond the  words and not a single word in this goes  to waste um and if you're paying  attention and like i said this is  probably why you want to read it through  a few times i actually read it through a  couple times um for this review you will  start to notice like all this an entire  wealth of like little clues and reveals  and all these interlinked details  between the stories and kind of like  foreshadowing of later events that are  sort of like flitting around like behind  you know behind the lines of text in  there and it's just really really great  like the way everything is just so  intricately like locked together but a  lot of times you really have to kind of  like read it just to kind of appreciate  how like i said how  deeply connected like all of these  people's stories are and  a lot of stuff like i said is  implication like some things that you're  kind of like suggested in this story  like you don't really figure out how  that pans out until later on and then  you're like oh my gosh that goes back to  that story like that guy and everything  like that so that's like really really  cool now i realize that this review is a  little bit shorter than what i would  normally do  but the book itself is so short that i'm  kind of hesitant to say any more about  it because i think that if you're going  to come to this you should kind of  experience it for yourself without  really knowing because like i said i  don't really have any preconceptions i  actually didn't even know that it was  that short or that or that it was uh  constructed in that way so it was kind  of like a nice surprise see you know  even though i read it for the first time  in less than an hour uh you know because  like i said it's only about 40 pages  it's actually sort of lingered in my  mind since then uh almost like this kind  of creepy but still like alluring  nightmare that you can't shake you know  what i mean it's that kind of you know  it just kind of sticks with you in that  way just the whole  i don't know just the whole aesthetic  the whole mood of it i read an interview  with christina mirzoy in cemetery dance  and she actually stated that at the  moment she's working on another english  language fantasy novella  um based around a particular fairy tale  like a reimagining of a particular fairy  tale and i have to say that you know  just going by this i'm really excited to  see you know to to read more from her um  she has a very singular voice and she  has  you know a really like majestic command  of the english language and kind of  putting across a mood she's just really  really good at that and doing that in a  really spare you know way where she  doesn't need like a whole bunch of  flowery language because all of her  words  you know kind of suggest a lot of other  things you know what i mean she's just  almost like a poet in that way  um so i definitely think that she's  gonna be a person to watch in the dark  fiction sphere in uh many years to come  so if you have kindle unlimited like i  said it's very short it's only 40 pages  it'll probably take you could probably  read it in afternoon easily and it's  really if you're really into fairy tales  um or mythological type tales things  like that then you will probably really  dig this i just really liked the whole  the whole mood that it gave me the whole  vibe it's just really really good and i  would really like recommend checking it  out so as i said sorry this one's a  little bit short but you know that's  kind of the book was short and i didn't  really want to spoil a lot of the stuff  because a lot of the fun of this one i  think is discovering like all of the  connections between all the stories and  all the characters and i don't really  want to ruin that for anybody so that  will do it for this tomes of terror and  i will see you guys on the next one bye

